--TEST--
A direct `compareTo` call through `Comparable` throws a `LogicError` when the receiver and the argument have different run-time classes, before `compareTo` runs, as an ordering does — `rule:classes/comparable-is-same-class-only`
--FILE--
<?nvs
interface Money extends Comparable {}

class Price implements Money {
    public function constructor(public int $cents) {}

    public function compareTo(self $other): int {
        return $this->cents <=> $other->cents;
    }
}

class Label implements Comparable {
    public function constructor(public string $text) {}

    public function compareTo(self $other): int {
        echo "Label::compareTo ran\n";
        return 0;
    }
}

class Coin implements Money {
    public function constructor(public int $value) {}

    public function compareTo(self $other): int {
        return $this->value <=> $other->value;
    }
}

// Two objects of the same class compare as `compareTo` says.
Comparable $cheap = new Price(100);
echo $cheap->compareTo(new Price(250)), "\n";

// The receiver is a `Price`, and the argument is a `Label`.
try {
    echo $cheap->compareTo(new Label("tea")), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo "1: ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// The receiver is a `Label`, and the argument is a `Price` in a variable.
Comparable $label = new Label("tea");
try {
    echo $label->compareTo($cheap), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo "2: ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// The call goes through an interface that extends `Comparable`.
Money $coin = new Coin(5);
try {
    echo $coin->compareTo(new Price(5)), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo "3: ", $e->message, "\n";
}
echo $coin->compareTo(new Coin(1)), "\n";
// covers: lang:classes/comparable
--EXPECT--
-1
1: `compareTo` compares two objects of different classes. Both objects must have the same class.
2: `compareTo` compares two objects of different classes. Both objects must have the same class.
3: `compareTo` compares two objects of different classes. Both objects must have the same class.
1
